\name{nutrigon_fit}
\alias{nutrigon_fit}
\title{Fit a quadratic response surface and locate the nutritional optimum}
\usage{
nutrigon_fit(data, x_col, y_col, z_col, design_id_col = NULL, peak = TRUE,
  n_cloud = 500, n_reps = 5, region_quantile = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{data}{A data frame containing nutrient intake and trait columns.}

\item{x_col}{Name of the protein (x-axis nutrient) column.}

\item{y_col}{Name of the carbohydrate (y-axis nutrient) column.}

\item{z_col}{Name of the trait (response) column.}

\item{design_id_col}{Name of a column identifying distinct design points
(e.g. treatment, or rail x concentration combination). Used for
within-design-point bootstrap resampling in \code{\link{nutrigon_compare}}.
If \code{NULL}, plain case-level resampling is used there instead.}

\item{peak}{\code{TRUE} (default) to locate the maximum; \code{FALSE} for
the minimum.}

\item{n_cloud}{Number of candidate points for the Monte Carlo fallback.}

\item{n_reps}{Number of MC draws (the final region is the union of all
draws).}

\item{region_quantile}{Top (or bottom) quantile defining the peak region.
Default: 0.95 for peaks, 0.05 for valleys.}
}
\value{
An object of class \code{"nutrigon_fit"}, a list with components:
\describe{
  \item{optimum}{Data frame with \code{x}, \code{y}, \code{theta} (angle
    from origin, degrees), and \code{used_fallback} (logical).}
  \item{model}{The \code{lm()} object for the fitted quadratic.}
  \item{data}{The cleaned input data.}
  \item{hull}{Design hull vertices (for plotting).}
  \item{region}{The top/bottom quantile region (for plotting).}
  \item{elongation}{Curvature elongation ratio (eigenvalue ratio of the
    Hessian).}
}
}
\description{
Fits a full quadratic response surface (\code{z ~ x + y + x^2 + y^2 + x:y})
to trait data collected under the Geometric Framework for Nutrition, and
locates the nutritional optimum (peak or valley) of the fitted surface.
The optimum is found in closed form when the fitted surface has the
correct curvature and the stationary point falls inside the convex hull
of the design; otherwise a Monte Carlo fallback samples points inside the
hull and takes the top (or bottom) quantile region.
}
\examples{
data(lee_drosophila)
fit_lifespan <- nutrigon_fit(lee_drosophila, "protein_eaten", "carb_eaten",
                              "lifespan", design_id_col = "treatment")
print(fit_lifespan)

}
\seealso{
\code{\link{nutrigon_compare}}, \code{\link{nutrigon_elongation}}
}
